w what's going on  inside the head of president putin um i  i think the the explanation given is the  most plausible that they clearly  have not had the early military victory  that they hoped they had hoped i think  to take keeve install the puppet  government and basically declare victory  uh the ukrainian fierce resistance at a  huge cost but with great bravery has  stopped that and i think indeed they are  falling back perhaps to a military  outcome which can be described as a  victory that's that's what it looks like  but listen uh there could be many twists  and turns in this before  we see anything like a ceasefire or a  cessation of hostilities so i think we  need to be very very careful in in in  predictions because quite a few of them  have proven to be  misplaced in the early stages of this  conflict and even more recently so i  think it is the most plausible  explanation but uh i wouldn't say that  uh mr putin has completely given up on  the idea perhaps of  of a wider military victory or perhaps  linking uh  the eastern provinces with uh with  crimea through down through the south of  ukraine  there are probably a number of different  ideas circulating in in russian military  circles and it's a question of what they  think they can they can they can win at  and this is probably going to be tested  now in the next few weeks peter you were  nodding through that um do you get the  feeling that as far as negotiations are  concerned there's really not much point  in trying to  work out whether or not negotiations are  actually working until we get to the  point that our previous two guests have  talked about and russia has got what it  regards as a definitive military victory  indeed you know i would i would uh break  my answer into two components first of  all i would corroborate what the with  the  david has just said uh that you know  putin has not only not been dissuaded  from his uh idea to continue this war in  ukraine concentrate on the donbass i i'm 